                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "will",
                    "title": "will: decisions, offers and predictions",
                    "explanation": "«Will» («'ll») + verbo en forma base se usa para:\n- decisiones que tomamos en el momento: «I'll go and ask.»\n- ofrecimientos: «I'll pay for the coffees.»\n- promesas: «I won't forget the tickets again!»\n- predicciones u opiniones sobre el futuro: «I think it will be busy.»\n\nLa negativa es «won't» («will not») y la pregunta, «Will…?»:\n- «Will it be busy?»\n\nCompáralo con «be going to», que expresa un plan ya decidido:\n- «I'm going to book the tickets tonight.» (lo pensé antes)\n- «I'll pay.» (lo decido ahora)",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Uso",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Decisión en el momento",
                          "I'll go and ask at the ticket office."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Ofrecimiento",
                          "I'll pay for the coffees."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Promesa",
                          "I won't forget the tickets again!"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Predicción",
                          "I think it will be quite busy."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Negativa",
                          "We won't find three seats together."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Pregunta",
                          "Will it be busy?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Plan decidido (going to)",
                          "I'm going to book the tickets tonight."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Don't panic. I'll go and ask.",
                      "It will cost ten pounds each.",
                      "I'll pay. It was my mistake.",
                      "We won't find three seats together."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "movement_prepositions",
                    "title": "Prepositions of movement: into, out of, across, through",
                    "explanation": "Las preposiciones de movimiento dicen hacia dónde va algo o alguien. Suelen ir con verbos como «go», «walk», «run», «get» o «come».\n\n«Into» / «out of»: entrar en / salir de un espacio:\n- «We went into the station.»\n- «She got out of the taxi.»\n\n«On» / «off» con transportes grandes (autobús, tren, avión); con coches y taxis, «get into» / «get out of»:\n- «get on the train», «get off the bus»\n\n«Across» (de un lado al otro, cruzando) y «through» (a través de, por dentro):\n- «Walk across the bridge.»\n- «Go through the gate.»\n\nOtras: «up» / «down» (subir / bajar), «along» (a lo largo de), «past» (por delante de), «towards» (hacia), «from… to…» (de… a…).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Preposición",
                        "Ejemplo",
                        "En español"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "into / out of",
                          "Run into the station!",
                          "entra corriendo en la estación"
                        ],
                        [
                          "on / off",
                          "Get off the train at Paddington.",
                          "bájate del tren en Paddington"
                        ],
                        [
                          "across",
                          "Walk across the bridge.",
                          "cruza el puente"
                        ],
                        [
                          "through",
                          "Go through the ticket barrier.",
                          "pasa por la barrera"
                        ],
                        [
                          "up / down",
                          "Go down the stairs to platform five.",
                          "baja las escaleras"
                        ],
                        [
                          "from … to",
                          "The train goes from Bristol to London.",
                          "de Bristol a Londres"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We ran into the station at nine o'clock.",
                      "Go down the stairs and across the bridge to platform five.",
                      "Get off the train at London Paddington.",
                      "She got out of the taxi and walked towards the ticket office."
                    ]
                  }
